          "type": "turn",
          "question": "possibly nothing but my son ate a peanut bar and now his mouth feels funny and hes making a whistling sound when he breathes in",
          "answer": "**Call emergency services now.** Do not wait to see whether symptoms settle. If your son has a prescribed epinephrine auto-injector, use it now, following the device's instructions. Then keep him under emergency care. (Source: red-flag-anaphylaxis, and the urgency rule table from assess_risk.)\n\nA whistling sound when breathing in, plus mouth discomfort after eating a food that may contain peanut, can be a sign of anaphylaxis. This is a severe allergic reaction that can become life-threatening quickly. Guidance says to suspect it when symptoms start within minutes to a couple of hours of exposure. (red-flag-anaphylaxis)\n\nGet help immediately if you notice any of these:\n\n- **Breathing:** breathlessness, noisy high-pitched breathing, gasping, skin pulling in between or under the ribs, or too breathless to talk or feed. Blue or gray lips or face are also emergency signs. (red-flag-severe-breathing-difficulty)\n- **Swelling:** of the lips, tongue, or throat, a tight throat, or a hoarse voice. (red-flag-anaphylaxis)\n- **Swallowing:** difficulty swallowing. (red-flag-anaphylaxis)\n- **Pale skin, faintness, or a weak, fast pulse:** signs of circulation trouble. (red-flag-anaphylaxis)\n- **Drowsiness or confusion** in a child who is struggling to breathe. (red-flag-severe-breathing-difficulty)\n\nDrooling is not described in the passages I have, so I cannot say how it fits in. Treat it as a reason to call emergency services now if it appears with any of the signs above.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
